WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:06.400
Film Courage: When a screenwriter finishes
a screenplay, is there a mental or physical

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:20.369
checklist that they should check of items?

00:00:20.369 --> 00:00:26.269
Kathie Fong Yoneda: I think it’s a good
idea to have a checklist or a chart.

00:00:26.269 --> 00:00:32.279
Certainly sending it out to a consultant or
somebody (a professional in the industry)

00:00:32.279 --> 00:00:39.550
is always very helpful because it gives you
an idea from somebody who has been in the

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:44.949
industry a long time what is it that might,
what are the little things and some of the

00:00:44.949 --> 00:00:48.699
bigger things that might keep it from moving
ahead .

00:00:48.699 --> 00:00:59.559
I 
think one of the things that bothers me is

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:05.140
when people really haven’t taken the time
to proof (I’m not kidding), that’s a major

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:07.200
thing.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:14.990
When I was an executive I also had cohorts
in my office who also execs and if they found

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:21.710
more than three mistakes in the first 20 pages
they would stop reading and throw it in the

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:22.780
trash.

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:24.659
I hate to say that but it happens.

00:01:24.659 --> 00:01:28.140
That really honest-to-God that does happen.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:32.980
On the other hand, give me a reason to say
yes.

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:41.360
So make sure that the structure of your screenplay
is such that right off the bat it catches

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:46.240
your attention, that you’ve got characters
that are so relatable that you’re going

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:53.790
to want to see them and see them either want
to succeed or to see the antagonist fail.

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:59.060
That’s one of the things I see is that a
lot of times people will pay more attention

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:05.450
to the hero and the unfortunate thing is they
don’t set up the antagonist well enough

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:11.880
that it gives the hero enough contrast and
interest.

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:19.410
That’s the big thing that I see a lot in
the things that I’ve read is that whoever

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:21.990
he is up against.

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:28.110
It doesn’t have to be a person, it could
be the elements like a storm or something

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:32.310
or it could just be himself.

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:35.970
You don’t see enough contrast in what’s
going on.

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:40.680
And so I think that’s another big mistake
that people make and that they should take

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:45.570
a look at it and have somebody read it and
ask that question.

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:48.140
Film Courage: Is my antagonist strong enough?

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:49.140
Kathie: Yes.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:55.150
Do you understand what’s at stake and do
you feel that the hero has worked hard enough

00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:56.510
to achieve that success?

00:02:56.510 --> 00:03:02.670
Film Courage: What’s the difference between
getting coverage and consultation?

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:08.190
Kathie: A lot of people say “I’m going
to get coverage on this.”

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:10.470
So they will send it out.

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:18.110
Coverage is something that we use in the studio
just so the executive will know what is the

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:22.710
story about because they sometimes don’t
have time to read the whole story.

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:27.270
It takes about an hour and a half to read
a script and with a 1,000 scripts coming in

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:29.490
every month it’s a lot of reading.

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:33.360
So that’s why they have story departments
who do the reading.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:36.470
So coverage is just like a book report.

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:42.780
It has a little longline in the front which
is one or two sentences which tells you the

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:45.250
general idea of what the story is about.

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:50.441
Then it’s basically just telling you the
story just like a regular book report, this

00:03:50.441 --> 00:03:54.460
is what happens and it’s usually done in
chronological order and then it usually tells

00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:59.960
you a little but (there will be box scores
or something which will tell you okay is this

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:00.960
a big budget?

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:03.700
Where does this fall on the budget?

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:08.500
How would you grade it in terms of do you
think the dialogue was well-written?

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:14.390
Do you think the characters have a story worth
telling?

00:04:14.390 --> 00:04:17.970
It will have those kind of little boxes and
you mark them off.

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:21.709
It usually says “Excellent, Good, Bad.

00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:23.830
Fair, Poor.”

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:30.890
And they just check those off and they just
write this little thing “I feel that the

00:04:30.890 --> 00:04:36.120
character (the hero) is too weak and there’s
just not enough interest in here for us.

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:40.690
There’s not enough at stake for us to really
root for him.

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:43.410
So they might give a few things.

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:50.810
What a consultation does they not only read,
they go through and they usually tell you

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:55.250
what they feel does not work.

00:04:55.250 --> 00:05:05.010
They get into details - your dialogue is too
on the nose or you mentioned this character

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:10.050
in the beginning but why do you include this
character if there’s no payoff at the end

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:12.180
for the character.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:21.040
Or they’ll say “Gosh, I like your hero
but for some reason I don’t quite understand

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:25.940
why your dialogue doesn’t match the personality
you’ve given him.”

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:27.720
That happens a lot too.

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:36.550
Where people put their own words into the
character rather than the character’s words

00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:38.500
into the dialogue.

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:39.970
And that happens a lot too.

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:45.800
Those are just some of the things that consultants
will find.

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:51.450
I think the good thing too about having a
consultant is that they will you like for

00:05:51.450 --> 00:06:00.560
right now if you send in something that has
to do with zombies or it has to do with dystopian

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:11.380
ideas a consultant will say “They don’t
mind but it has to have this, this and this.

00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:18.150
Zombies…we’ve had too many submissions
on that, the chances kind of go down if it’s

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:19.490
about zombies."

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:24.520
They will give you hopefully some up-to-date
information on what they’ve gleaned from

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:29.180
reading the trade papers and all that so that’s
what I do when I do a consultation.

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:38.030
I will be very upfront about what works and
doesn’t work not just about the writing

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:42.139
but about the script in general and how the
marketplace is now.

00:06:42.139 --> 00:06:47.680
Because I don’t want to ever lead them in
the wrong direction and I will be very upfront.

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:53.960
I will not be brutal, I try not to be brutal
because nobody deserves that.

00:06:53.960 --> 00:07:01.080
But I recently had someone send me a script
about zombies and I had to be very honest

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:02.080
about it.

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:07.710
And I said “You know, this would have been
a good script for people to consider three

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:10.290
years ago.

00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:17.240
Unfortunately I know for a fact that recently
we had such and such at the script fest and

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:21.389
I know that several agents have said that
they do not want to see any more zombie scripts

00:07:21.389 --> 00:07:23.880
because they feel that there are just too
many of them.

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:26.180
I mean you have to be honest about it.

00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:28.669
I don’t want to build up their hopes on
that.

00:07:28.669 --> 00:07:35.460
I’ve done things also where I have said
“Do you think you can make a transition

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:44.430
here where instead of zombies maybe it has
to do with something like that there are people

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:52.040
here who are actually aliens who have come
to earth or I try to come to a way where they

00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:58.419
can maybe change it to where the heart of
the story remains but the situation itself

00:07:58.419 --> 00:08:05.010
(like zombies) can be changed.

00:08:05.010 --> 00:08:06.740
Consultations differ a lot.

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:12.590
I try to do that if I can with mine and I
think a lot of my colleagues do to where we

00:08:12.590 --> 00:08:18.570
try to find out what is the best way with
what they are doing and letting them know

00:08:18.570 --> 00:08:24.680
what that is “This is what we liked about
it” And then letting them know “This is

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:28.270
what can be a problem.”

00:08:28.270 --> 00:08:35.830
And also along with it whatever new information
we can give them such as “We think that

00:08:35.830 --> 00:08:38.640
zombies have had their run.

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:44.640
We’ve been on panels and we’ve talked
to agents and producers and this is what seems.”

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:50.480
And I’ll even put this is some of the areas
that they’re looking at if they happen to

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:52.150
mention anything.

00:08:52.150 --> 00:08:58.980
Like they are looking for more action pieces
or whatever they are looking for.

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:06.090
I just try to give them additional information
about where they could take this project or

00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:11.750
where they could themselves start another
project, what area they might want to start

00:09:11.750 --> 00:09:11.990
in.

